Let $f_0,f_1 \in \mathcal{D}'(\mathbb{R})$. By \cite[Th. 5.1.6.]{duistermaat-FIOs}, there is a unique solution $u \in \mathcal{D}_\Sigma' (\mathbb{R}^2)$ to the Cauchy problem  egin{align} &\square_A u = 0 \quad 	ext{on} \quad \mathbb{R}^2, \ & u|_{t=0} = f_0 \ &\partial_t u |_{t=0} = f_1. \end{align} Moreover, the solution depends continuously on the data $(f_0,f_1)$. Indeed, the additional condition \cite[(5.1.10)]{duistermaat-FIOs} in the theorem is met. {Below we apply this with specific choice of $f_0$, $f_1$. The idea is that, with the right choice of Cauchy data, the solution of the Cauchy problem solves the initial-boundary value problem \eqref{ibvp11}-\eqref{ibvp44} away from $(0,\infty)	imes (-\infty,-s]$. By cutting off the component of $\operatorname{supp} (u)$ in $(0,\infty)	imes (-\infty,-s]$ we obtain a solution in the half-space $(0,\infty)	imes \mathbb{R}$. Hence, the Cauchy problem in $\mathbb{R}^2$ can be used to construct solution to the initial-boundary value problem \eqref{ibvp11}-\eqref{ibvp44} in the half-space.} Let $\phi \in C^\infty_c( \mathbb{R}) $ be a mollifier and denote $\phi_\varepsilon =  rac{1}{\varepsilon} \phi \left(  rac{x}{\varepsilon} ight)$. Let $u_\epsilon \in C^\infty(\mathbb{R}^2)$ be the {global} solution to the following Cauchy problem:  egin{align} &\square_A u_\varepsilon = 0 \quad 	ext{on} \quad \mathbb{R}^2, \ & u_\varepsilon |_{t=0} = (-1)^\mu \phi_\varepsilon * 	au^* f^{(-\mu)} , \ &\partial_t u_\varepsilon |_{t=0} = (-1)^\mu \phi_\varepsilon * 	au^* f^{(1-\mu)} . \end{align} where $	au : \mathbb{R} 	o \mathbb{R}$, $	au (x) := -x$, $f^{(0)} (x) := f(x) $, $f^{(1)} (x):= \partial_x f(x) $ and $f^{(-1)}$ is the forward propagating inverse derivative, defined by  egin{align} \langle f^{(-1)}, \phi angle := -\langle f, 	ilde\phi angle, \quad 	ilde\phi(x) := \int_{-\infty}^{x } \phi(r) dr , \end{align} for every $\phi \in C_c^\infty(\mathbb{R})$. Notice that this is well defined since $f$ is compactly supported. Moreover, $f^{(-\mu)} $ is supported in $(s,\infty)$. The solution $u_0$ associated with the data $(f_0,f_1) = ((-1)^\mu	au^*f^{(-\mu)}, (-1)^\mu 	au^*f^{(1-\mu)})$ coincides with the limit $ \lim_{\varepsilon 	o 0} u_\varepsilon$. { Let us shortly explain why we choose this wave. First, suppose we have a trivial profile $A=1$. Then the explicit expression for the wave $u_\varepsilon$ can be derived by integrating along the null lines $\{ x = c\pm t\}$ (cf. the local version of this argument below). In that case, we would find, after applying a cut-off, that $u_\varepsilon$ solves $\square_A u = 0$, $\partial^\mu_x u |_{x=0} = \phi_\varepsilon * f$ and $u = 0 = \partial_t u$ for $t<0$ for small $\varepsilon>0$. Taking the limit $\varepsilon 	o 0$ would therefore give the solution to \eqref{ibvp11}-\eqref{ibvp44} (even for $\delta= \infty $) by continuity. The mollification in the data was introduced so that the integration along null curves would be meaningful. Somewhat similar argument could be made directly without the mollification. Notice that the Cauchy problem itself does not require smoothness. Let us now return to the case where $A$ is non-trivial in $(\delta, \infty)$. Although $A=1$ holds near the t-axis $\{x = 0\}$, the preceding argument is not valid anymore. The issue is the possibility of echoes originating from the region $x>\delta$. Nevertheless, such echoes reach the point $x=0$ only after a delay: the initial wave must first propagate into the region, and any scattered signal must then return to $x=0$. Consequently, the boundary value $\partial^\mu_x u |_{x=0} = \phi_\varepsilon * f$ is preserved for early times $t>0$. More precisely, this holds for $t \in (-\infty,2\delta +s)$ provided that $\varepsilon>0$ is sufficiently small. } {Let us now do this in detail. As implied above,} the operator $\square_A$ reduces to the trivial wave operator ${ \square_1 =} \square = \partial_t^2 - \partial_x^2$ in the half space $ (-\infty,\delta ] 	imes \mathbb{R} $ for any sufficiently small $\delta >0$. It is easy to check that $ \square $ equals $ rac{1}{4} \partial_{0} \partial_{1}$ in the coordinates $(z_0,z_1) := (t-x, t+x)$. Integrating twice, one deduces that the general smooth solution to $ rac{1}{4} \partial_{0} \partial_{1} v = 0$ is \[ v(z_0,z_1) = F(z_0 ) + G(z_1) = (F \otimes 1) (z_0,z_1 ) + (1 \otimes G) (z_0,z_1) , \] where $F,G \in C^\infty(\mathbb{R})$. Moreover, if $ v_\varepsilon = F_\varepsilon \otimes 1 + 1 \otimes G_\varepsilon$ for $\varepsilon >0$ is a family of such solutions and the limit $\lim_{\varepsilon 	o 0} v_\varepsilon$ exists in $\mathcal{D}'(\mathbb{R}^2)$, we may choose $F_\varepsilon $ and $G_\varepsilon $ such that they converge individually to some distributions $ F_0 $ and $G_0 $. Hence, { the global solution $u_\varepsilon \in C^\infty ( \mathbb{R}^2)$ must be of the form}  egin{equation} u_\varepsilon (x,t) = F_\varepsilon (t-x ) + G_\varepsilon (t+x) \end{equation} in $ (-\infty,\delta] 	imes \mathbb{R} $ for some $F_\varepsilon,G_\varepsilon $ that converge in $ \mathcal{D}'(\mathbb{R})$ as $\varepsilon 	o 0$. {Notice that this expression holds even though the functions $F_\varepsilon,G_\varepsilon$ cannot be solved from local information alone.} Formally, we have  egin{equation} u_0 (x,t) = F_0 (t-x ) + G_0 (t+x) \end{equation} for $(x,t) \in (-\infty,\delta) 	imes \mathbb{R}$. For the rigorous expression, define $ho_\pm : \mathbb{R}^2 	o \mathbb{R} $, $ho_\pm (x,t) := t\pm x$ and extend the pull-backs $ho^*_\pm$ into continuous operators $ho_\pm^* : \mathcal{D}'( \mathbb{R} ) 	o \mathcal{D}'( \mathbb{R}^2 )$ by applying the last identity in \[ \langle ho^*v , \phi angle = \int_{\mathbb{R}^2} v(t\pm x) \phi(x,t) dt dx = \int_{\mathbb{R}^2} v(s ) \phi(x,s \mp x ) ds dx . \] That is, $ho_\pm^*v = (\sigma_\pm )_* ( 1 \otimes v )$ where $\sigma_\pm : \mathbb{R}^2 	o \mathbb{R}^2$ is the diffeomorphism $\sigma_\pm (x,s) = (x,s \mp x)$. The identity \eqref{wqi} then reads \[ u_\varepsilon = ho_-^* F_\varepsilon + ho_+^* G_\varepsilon , \quad 	ext{in } \quad (-\infty,\delta] 	imes \mathbb{R} \] and taking the limit yields  egin{equation} u_0 = ho_-^* F_0 + ho_+^* G_0, \quad 	ext{in } \quad (-\infty,\delta) 	imes \mathbb{R} \end{equation} by the continuity of $ ho_\pm^* $. This is the formal identity \eqref{wqi1} in a rigorous form. The two terms, $ ho_-^* F_0 \in \mathcal{D}_{\Sigma^ightarrow}' ( (-\infty,\delta) 	imes \mathbb{R} )$ and $ ho_+^* G_0 \in \mathcal{D}_{\Sigma^\leftarrow}' ( (-\infty,\delta) 	imes \mathbb{R} )$, represent the components of $u_0$ propagating in the {respective} characteristic component \[ \Sigma^{ightarrow} := \{ (x,t, - \lambda , \lambda) : \lambda \in \dot{\mathbb{R}}\}, \quad \Sigma^{\leftarrow} := \{ (x,t, \lambda , \lambda) : \lambda \in \dot{\mathbb{R}}\} \] towards the infinities $( \pm \infty , \infty)$ (i.e. to the right or left). In fact, we may split \[ u_0 = u_0^ightarrow + u_0^\leftarrow, \quad u_0^{ightarrow} \in \mathcal{D}_{\Sigma^{ightarrow }}' ( \mathbb{R}^2 ), \quad u_0^{\leftarrow} \in \mathcal{D}_{\Sigma^{\leftarrow }}' ( \mathbb{R}^2 ) \] in the whole $\mathbb{R}^2$ such that $u_0^ightarrow = ho_-^* F_0$ and $u_0^\leftarrow = ho_+^* G_0$ in $(-\infty , \delta) 	imes \mathbb{R}$.  egin{figure}[H]  egin{center}  egin{tikzpicture}[scale = 0.8]  ill[lightgray] (1,5) -- (-5,5) -- (-5,-5) -- (1,-5) --cycle;  ill[gray] (-1,0) -- (4,5) -- (4,-5) -- cycle; \draw[->] (-6,0) -- (5,0) node[anchor=north west] {$x$ axis}; \draw[->] (-0.5,-6) -- (-0.5,7) node[anchor=south] {$t$ axis}; \draw[dashed] (-1,-6) -- (-1,6) node[anchor=south east] {$x=-s$ }; \draw[dashed] (1,-6) -- (1,6) node[anchor=south] {$x=\delta$ }; 
ode at (2.5, -1) (a) {\large $W$}; 
ode at (-3, -4) (a) {\large $(-\infty, \delta)	imes \mathbb{R}$}; \end{tikzpicture} \caption{A visualization of the sets $(-\infty, \delta) 	imes \mathbb{R}$ and $W$. The light gray area represents the half space $(-\infty, \delta) 	imes \mathbb{R} $. The wedge $W$ is the darker shape on the right.} \end{center} \end{figure} Let $s>0 $ be such that $ 	ext{supp} (f) \subset (s ,\infty), $ as in the assumptions. Then the Cauchy data of $u_0$ is supported in $(-\infty ,-s)$ and $u_0$ vanishes in the wedge $W:= \{ (x,t) : |t| - s < x \}$ (see Figure ef{captionW}) by \cite[Th. 5.1.6]{duistermaat-FIOs}. This implies that $u_0^ightarrow = - u_0^\leftarrow $ in $W $. Since the wave front sets of these terms do not overlap, they must both be smooth in $W$ for this to hold. In $W \cap (0,\delta) 	imes \mathbb{R}$, they then reduce into $u_0^ightarrow(x,t) = F_0(t-x)$ and $u_0^\leftarrow(x,t) = G_0(t+x)$, where $F_0$ and $G_0$ are smooth. In the coordinates $z_0 = t-x$ and $z_1 = t+x$, this reads $u_0^ightarrow(z_0,z_1) = F_0(z_0)$ and $u_0^\leftarrow(z_0,z_1) = G_0(z_1)$. Setting $F_0(z_0) = - G_0(z_1)$ in $W \cap (0,\delta) 	imes \mathbb{R}$ implies $F_0(z_0) = C = - G_0(z_1)$ there. We can assume $C=0$ by redefining the functions $F_0 $ and $G_0 $. Indeed, the opposing constants $\pm C$ may be added to them without changing $u_0$.  egin{figure}[ht]  egin{center}  egin{tikzpicture}[scale = 0.8]  ill[lightgray] (1,5) -- (-5,5) -- (-5,-5) -- (1,-5) --cycle;  ill[gray] (-1,0) -- (4,5) -- (4,-5) -- cycle;  ill[darkgray] (1,-2) -- (1,2) -- (-2,5) -- (-5,5) -- (-5,4) -- cycle; \draw[->] (-6,0) -- (5,0) node[anchor=north west] {$x$ axis}; \draw[->] (-0.5,-6) -- (-0.5,7) node[anchor=south] {$t$ axis}; \draw[dashed] (1,-6) -- (1,6) node[anchor=south] {$x=\delta$ }; \draw[dashed] (-1,-6) -- (-1,6) node[anchor=south east] {$x=-s$ }; \draw[dotted, thick] (-1,0) -- (1,2) ; \draw[dashed] (-6,0.5) -- (5,0.5) node[anchor=south west] {$t=s$ } ; \draw[dashed] (-6,2) -- (5,2) node[anchor=south west] {$t=s+\delta$ } ; \draw[dashed] (-6,3.5) -- (5,3.5) node[anchor=south west] {$t=s+2\delta$ } ; 
