ode[white] at (-2, 2.7) (a) {\large $X$}; \end{tikzpicture} \caption{A visualization of $X$ in relation to $W$ and $(-\infty, \delta)	imes \mathbb{R}$. The set $X$ is shown in dark gray. The medium and light gray areas represent $W$ and $(-\infty,\delta) 	imes \mathbb{R}$, respectively. } \end{center} \end{figure} With this choice, $u_0^\leftarrow = 0$ in $W \cap (0,\delta) 	imes \mathbb{R}$ and the invariance of $G_0(t+x)$ along the rays \[ R_c = \{ (x,t) \in (-\infty,\delta) 	imes \mathbb{R} : t + x =c\}, c \in \mathbb{R}, \] implies that $u_0^\leftarrow$ vanishes in \[ X:= \cup \{ R_c : c \in \mathbb{R}, \ R_c \cap W 
eq \emptyset\} = \{ (x,t) \in (-\infty,\delta) 	imes \mathbb{R} : -x-s < t < 2 \delta + s -x \}. \] This set is shown in Figure ef{captionX}. By \cite[Th. 5.1.6]{duistermaat-FIOs}, the solution to the Cauchy problem is unique in the wedge \[ V = \{ (x,t) \in (-\infty,\delta) {	imes \mathbb{R}} : x < -|t| + \delta\}. \] This set is shown in Figure ef{captionV}.  egin{figure}[ht]  egin{center}  egin{tikzpicture}[scale = 0.8]  ill[lightgray] (1,5) -- (-4,5) -- (-4,-5) -- (1,-5) --cycle;  ill[gray] (-1,0) -- (4,5) -- (4,-5) -- cycle;  ill[darkgray] (1,0) -- (-4,5) -- (-4,-5) -- cycle; \draw[->] (-5,0) -- (5,0) node[anchor=north west] {$x$ axis}; \draw[->] (-0.5,-6) -- (-0.5,6) node[anchor=south east] {$t$ axis}; \draw[dashed] (1,-6) -- (1,6) node[anchor=south] {$x=\delta$ }; 
ode[white] at (-2, -1) (a) {\large $V$}; 
ode at (2.5, -1) (a) {\large $W$}; \end{tikzpicture} \caption{A visualization of $V$ in relation to the sets $W$ and $(-\infty, \delta ) 	imes \mathbb{R}$. The wedge $V$ is shown in dark gray. The medium and light gray areas represent $W$ and $(-\infty,\delta) 	imes \mathbb{R}$, respectively. } \end{center} \end{figure} In our case, that solution is the function $(-1)^\mu ho^*_- f^{(-\mu)} $. Hence, $u_0 = (-1)^\mu ho^*_- f$ in $V$. This means that $u_0^ightarrow= (-1)^\mu ho^*_- f^{(-\mu)} $ and $u_0^\leftarrow = 0$ in $V$. On the other hand, $u_0^ightarrow= ho^*_- F_0$ holds in $(-\infty,\delta) 	imes \mathbb{R}$ and applying the invariance of $ho^*_- F_0$ along the rays $x-t =c$, we deduce that $u_0^ightarrow =(-1)^\mu ho^*_- f^{(-\mu)} $ holds in \[ X_{t > 0} := \{ (x,t) \in X : t > 0 \} \] too. Indeed, the rays from $V \cap X$ fill $X_{t > 0}$ and we obtain the extension of $u_0^ightarrow = (-1)^\mu ho^*_- f^{(-\mu)} $ from $V \cap X$ to this set by the invariance. Since we already know that $u_0^\leftarrow$ vanishes in $X$, we conclude that $u_0 = (-1)^\mu ho^*_- f^{(-\mu)} $ does not only hold in $V$ but also in $V \cup X_{t >0}$. Recall that by \cite[Th. 5.1.6.]{duistermaat-FIOs} the wave $u_0$ vanishes in $W \supset (0,\infty) 	imes \{0\}$. This further extends the identity $u_0 = (-1)^\mu ho^*_- f^{(-\mu)} $ to $V \cup X_{t >0} \cup W$. Considering $u_0$ as a distribution in $\mathcal{D}'((0,\infty) 	imes \mathbb{R})$, we may cut off everything below $W \cap (0,\infty) 	imes \mathbb{R}$ without conflicting $\square_A u_0 = 0$. Indeed, $\square_A u = 0$ in $(0,\infty) 	imes \mathbb{R}$ for $u := \chi u_0$, where $\chi \in C^\infty$ satisfies $\chi|_{t>-s/4} = 1$ and $\chi|_{t<-s/2} = 0$. Moreover, $u$ vanishes in $(0,\infty) 	imes (-\infty,0)$ since $u_0 = 0$ in $W$ and $\chi$ cuts off everything below it in $(0,\infty) 	imes \mathbb{R}$. As a conclusion, the identity $u = (-1)^\mu ho^*_- f^{(-\mu)} $ holds in \[ V \cup X_{t >0} \cup W \cup \ker \chi . \] This set contains an open neighbourhood of $\{0\} 	imes (-\infty, 2 \delta + s)$ and hence \[ \partial^\mu_x u|_{x=0} = \partial^\mu_x ( (-1)^\mu ho^*_- f^{(-\mu)} )|_{x=0} = f \] for $t<2 \delta + s$. { Finally, we note that the element $u=u_f$ above does not change if we choose different \ $s \in (0,\min \operatorname{supp}(f))$. Indeed, the Cauchy problem \eqref{cauchy1}-\eqref{cauchy3}, which was used to generate $u$, remain the same. Moreover, the linearity of $u$ in $f$ is also inherited from the Cauchy problem. }

